  ],
  "slug": "microbiome-16s-366c",
  "summary": "Perturbation-response data now anchor virtual cell models, prompting new scrutiny of whether benchmark gains reflect scientific value.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is the use of perturbation response data, spanning genetic perturbations and drug-induced transcriptomic and imaging profiles, as the substrate for predicting cellular outcomes. Model-building items include scPert, a Transformer fusing LLM embeddings, knowledge graphs and foundation-model contexts to predict single-cell responses to genetic perturbation, and HyperMap, a meta-learning framework that transfers perturbation atlas responses from few seeds. A second strand interrogates validity: additive baselines account for MULTI-evolve's multimutant predictions without evidence of epistasis learning, PerturbHD argues current benchmarks fail to measure scientific value, and MVCBench benchmarks 24 drug representation methods on about 1.1M profiles. What is new is pairing model development with evaluation rigor. Most items are preprints awaiting independent replication.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-04-29"
}
